{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-09-advanced-identities-conditions",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Advanced Logarithmic Identities and Conditions",
    "description": "Simplify cyclic and symmetric logarithmic expressions and solve conditions built from identities, averages, and arithmetic progressions of logs.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use the hardest chapter patterns as structure-recognition exercises. Each block starts with the cancellation idea, then tests it with fresh values or symbols. Keep the algebra compact and always point out the exact identity that makes the expression collapse.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 31,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Cover the harder objective patterns after the core laws are secure: conditions such as log x+log y=log(x+y), equality in the logarithmic average that forces x=y, cyclic reciprocal products, sums where denominators become log_a(abc), cyclic base ratios whose product is 1, and arithmetic-progression conditions on three logarithms. Include symmetric cancellation such as log(a^2/bc)+log(b^2/ac)+log(c^2/ab)=0 and the chapter-style relation log a=p, log b=q leading to log(a^p b^q)=p^2+q^2. Require learners to identify the cancellation structure before expanding."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's finish with logarithm identities that look longer than they really are.\n\nThe trick is usually to spot a product, a cycle, or a symmetry before doing any detailed calculation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose log x + log y = log(x+y).\n\nThe left side combines to log(xy), so the arguments must satisfy:\nxy=x+y.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Solve for y:\nxy−y=x\ny(x−1)=x\ny=x/(x−1).\n\nThe logarithms have turned into an ordinary algebra condition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "If positive x and y satisfy log x + log y = log(x+y) and x=3, what is y?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "3/2"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "2"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "1/2"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "3"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
